Interface IIOModule (AAMotion; AAMotion C#): IIOModule interface definition, used to describe the parameters and states related to the IO module
Signature: interface AAMotion.IIOModule

Members:
- property AAMotion.AADynamicValueArray AInDB { get; }
  Analog input deadband (unit: mV). If the input voltage is between (-AInDB) and AInDB, the reading is 0; If it exceeds AInDB, the reading is (actual voltage - AInDB); If it is below (-AInDB), the reading is (actual voltage + AInDB). - Old firmware (int type): Integer deadband values in millivolts. - New firmware (float type): Float deadband values in millivolts. Each element is a double, whatever the controller's parameter type. AInOffSet , AInGain , AInFilt , AInPort
- property AAMotion.AADynamicValueArray AInFilt { get; }
  Analog input filter coefficient array. AInFilt[i] determines the filter coefficient for the i-th analog input. - Old firmware (int type): Integer coefficients (65536 = no filtering, i.e., C1=1). - New firmware (float type): Direct float coefficient values. Each element is a double, whatever the controller's parameter type. AInOffSet , AInDB , AInGain , AInPort
- property AAMotion.AADynamicValueArray AInGain { get; }
  Analog input gain array. - Old firmware (int type): Actual gain = AInGain / 65536. When gain is 1, AInGain=65536. - New firmware (float type): Direct float value. When gain is 1, AInGain=1.0. Each element is a double, whatever the controller's parameter type. AInOffSet , AInDB , AInFilt , AInPort
- property AAMotion.AAValueArray<System.Double> AInMode { get; }
  Analog input mode configuration array. Each element defines the function of the corresponding analog input point. Common function values: 0 - General input 1 - Velocity command 2 - Current command 3 - Force feedback 4 - Force command 5 - Joystick input 6 - Torque compensation 7 - Negative torque limit 8 - Positive torque limit 9 - Speed feedback Others - Unknown
- property AAMotion.AADynamicValueArray AInOffSet { get; }
  Analog input offset (unit: mV). The value of AInOffset[i] will be added to the actual input voltage. - Old firmware (int type): Integer offset values in millivolts. - New firmware (float type): Float offset values in millivolts. Each element is a double, whatever the controller's parameter type. AInDB , AInGain , AInFilt , AInPort
- property AAMotion.AADynamicValueArray AInPort { get; }
  Read-only array containing processed and raw values of analog input ports. The first half: processed values (filtered, offset, deadband, gain applied) for each channel; The second half: raw input values for each channel (unprocessed). - Old firmware (int type): Integer values representing raw ADC counts. - New firmware (float type): Float values representing actual voltage/processed values. Each element is a double, whatever the controller's parameter type. AInFilt, AInDB, AInGain, AInOffSet
- property AAMotion.AAValueArray<System.Double> AOutGain { get; }
  Analog output gain, a plain multiplier: AOutGain[i] scales the i-th analog output together with the parameter selected by AOutMode. Reported by firmware with float analog IO. Firmware without it reports AOutShifts at the same CAN code instead — a different parameter, not another name for this one — and on such a controller reading or writing an output, AOutGain[i] with i from 1, raises IndexOutOfRangeException before anything is sent. AOutShifts , AOutMode , AOutPort
- property AAMotion.AAValueArray<System.Double> AOutMode { get; }
  AOutMode determines the parameter monitored by each analog output channel. AOutMode[i] specifies the parameter reflected by the i-th analog output. The structure of AOutMode (32-bit integer): - Bits 0~9 (LSB, 10 bits): CAN code of the parameter - Bits 10~12 (3 bits): Axis number (0 is the first axis) - Bits 16~31 (16 bits): Array index (ignored if not used) AOutPort , AOutOffset , AOutGain
- property AAMotion.AAValueArray<System.Double> AOutOffset { get; }
  Analog output offset (unit: mV). The value of AOutOffset[i] will be added to the actual output voltage for calibration or adjustment.
- property AAMotion.AAValueArray<System.Double> AOutPort { get; }
  Direct analog output (unit: mV). When AOutMode[i]=0, the value of AOutPort[i] is directly output to the i-th analog output. AOutMode , AOutGain
- property AAMotion.AAValueArray<System.Double> AOutShifts { get; }
  Analog output shift exponent: the i-th analog output is scaled by 2^AOutShifts[i], so a negative value divides and a positive one multiplies. Reported by firmware without float analog IO. Firmware with it reports AOutGain at the same CAN code instead, which carries a plain multiplier rather than an exponent — the two are not interchangeable, and 2 here means x4 where 2 there means x2. On a controller that reports AOutGain reading or writing an output, AOutShifts[i] with i from 1, raises IndexOutOfRangeException before anything is sent. AOutGain , AOutMode , AOutPort
- property System.Int32 BiDirConfig { get; set; }
  Input bidirectional differential direction configuration. Selects between input and output mode for each input independently. 0 - input, 1 - output.
- property System.Int32 DInFilt { get; set; }
  Pulse/direction input filter, in sampling cycles (80MHz). The input value is updated only if the result is consistent for DInFilt consecutive samples.
- property System.Int32 DInLog { get; set; }
  Digital input logic inversion control. Each bit corresponds to an input point: 0 - normal logic (On returns 1, Off returns 0), 1 - inverted logic (On returns 0, Off returns 1). DInPort,DInFilt,DInMode
- property System.Int32 DInLogHigh { get; set; }
- property AAMotion.AAValueArray<System.Double> DInMode { get; }
  Discrete input mode configuration array. Each element defines the function of the corresponding input point. Common function values (see documentation): 0 - General input 2 - Servo enable 3 - Start motion 4 - Stop motion 5 - Clear input pulse 7 - Clear fault/alarm 8 - Abort motion 9 - RLS (reverse limit) 10 - FLS (forward limit) 11 - Torque limit on 12 - Activate dynamic break 13 - Activate motor brake 14 - Change control filter set 15 - Add velocity filter 16 - Velocity/Position mode switch 17 - Velocity/Current mode switch 18 - Position/Current mode switch 19 - Clear absolute encoder 20 - Change speed 21 - Home 22 - Mode switch between Position Operation Mode and Force Operation Mode 23 - Hall A 24 - Fault input 25 - HomingOn input DOutMode, DInPort, DInLog, DOutPort
- property System.Int32 DInPort { get; }
  Gets the status of the digital input port. The return value is a decimal integer, each bit represents the status of a digital input point. DInLog,DInFilt,DInMode,DInPortHigh
- property System.Int32 DInPortHigh { get; }
  Gets the status of the high digital input port. The return value is a decimal integer, each bit represents the status of a high input point.
- method System.Boolean DOutClearBit(System.Int32)
  Clear the specified digital output bit DOutPort
- property System.Int32 DOutLog { get; set; }
  Digital output logic inversion control. Each bit corresponds to an output point: 0 - normal logic (DOutPort=1 outputs "On", 0 outputs "Off"), 1 - inverted logic (1 outputs "Off", 0 outputs "On"). Note: The 0th bit (LSB) of DOutLog corresponds to output 1. DOutMode, DOutMode, DOutPort

Interface ISingleAxis (AAMotion; AAMotion C#): Single axis interface, defines all properties and methods of a single axis motion controller. This is a sub-interface of IVector.
Signature: interface AAMotion.ISingleAxis

Members:
- property AAMotion.AADynamicValueArray AInGain { get; }
  AInGain - Analog input gain array. The indexer yields each element as a double, whatever the controller's parameter type.
- method System.Boolean Abort()
  Emergency stop all motion.
- property System.Double AbsTrgt { get; set; }
  AbsTrgt is the absolute target for point-to-point and repetitive motions. AbsTrgt defines the desired target position at the end of the motion regardless of the current position, readable and writable.
- property System.Double Accel { get; set; }
  Acceleration, readable and writable. Accel is the acceleration used for all motions, in user units/sec².
- property System.Int32 AccelFact { get; set; }
  AccelFact is the acceleration factor for the CNC commutation method.
- method System.Boolean AutoExec(System.Boolean)
  Enable or disable auto-execution of IDE program on power-up.
- method System.Void AutoPhase(System.Nullable<System.Int32> = null)
  Perform Auto-Phase operation.
- property System.Int32 AuxEncDir { get; set; }
  Auxiliary encoder direction, readable and writable. 0: Forward, 1: Reverse.
- property System.Int32 AuxEncFilt { get; set; }
  Auxiliary encoder filter, readable and writable.
- property System.Int32 AuxEncType { get; set; }
  Auxiliary encoder feedback type, readable and writable. 0: Unknown 1: Incremental encoder 2: Sin/Cos 3: Endat 4: SSI 5: Nikon 17 bit encoder
- property System.Double AuxIndexPos { get; }
  Last auxiliary encoder index position. Only valid for incremental encoders. Note: To ensure correct index detection, encoder speed must be less than controller sampling frequency. The value is a double and carries any User-Units scaling AAComm applied to the reply.
- property System.Int32 AuxIndexStat { get; }
  The AuxIndexStat holds the current status ("0" or "1") of the auxiliary encoder index input (for incremental encoder only).
- property System.Double AuxModRev { get; set; }
  AuxModRev is used for the auxiliary encoder's modulo mode, readable and writable.
- property System.Double AuxPos { get; }
  AuxPos reports the auxiliary encoder position reading in auxiliary user units (AuxUsrUnits). The value is a double and carries any User-Units scaling AAComm applied to the reply.
- property System.Int32 AuxUsrUnits { get; set; }
  Auxiliary user units, readable and writable. AuxUsrUnits represents the conversion ratio between the target unit and the encoder count.
- property System.Double AuxVel { get; }
  AuxVel reports the velocity of the auxiliary encoder in auxiliary user units. The auxiliary velocity is reported in user-units / second. The value is a double and carries any User-Units scaling AAComm applied to the reply.
- method System.Boolean Begin()
  Start axis motion.
- property System.Int32 BeginOnToPos { get; set; }
  Start after in-position, readable and writable. When set to 1, automatically start the next operation after reaching the target position.
- property System.Double CNCAAccel { get; }
  CNCA acceleration for the CNC commutation method.
- property System.Double CNCADecel { get; }
  CNCA deceleration for the CNC commutation method.
- property System.Int32 CNCAEncFactDn { get; set; }
  CNCAEncFactDn is the denominator of the encoder factor for the CNC commutation method.
- property System.Int32 CNCAEncFactNu { get; set; }
  CNCAEncFactNu is the numerator of the encoder factor for the CNC commutation method.
- property System.Int32 CNCAEndSegMod { get; set; }
  CNCAEndSegMod is the end segment mode for the CNC commutation method.
- property System.Double CNCAJerk { get; }
  CNCA jerk for the CNC commutation method.
- property System.Double CNCASpeed { get; }
  CNCA speed for the CNC commutation method.
- property System.Int32 CNCASpeedPer { get; set; }
  CNCASpeedPer is the speed percentage for the CNC commutation method.
- method System.Boolean CommitMotion()
  Commit the Sine PTP parameters currently written to the axis so the controller splices them onto the motion already running (an on-the-fly change). The controller validates the new profile within 16 control cycles (about 1 ms) and then joins the two profiles; position and velocity stay continuous across the splice, acceleration does not. Every kinematic parameter is read as one complete set describing the new motion, so write PreCruiseSpd = 0 to drop a pre-cruise phase the new motion no longer needs. A non-zero RelTrgt is measured from where the axis is at the splice, not from where the motion began. The controller's command names the axes it commits by mask, so this sends ACommitMotion=<this axis's bit>; MotionController.CommitMotion(AxisRef) commits several axes in one synchronised step.
- method System.Void Commutated(System.Nullable<System.Int32> = null)
  Start commutation: the same command as AutoPhase. Unless ComtStatus[1] already reads commutated (as IsCommutated judges it) or 1 (commutation running), writes ComtMode[5] = 1282, which starts the commutation configured on the axis; it does not mark the axis commutated by itself. Poll IsCommutated for the result.
- property System.Double ComtAng { get; }
  ComtAng is the commutation angle in encoder counts, or the distance in encoder counts between the current position and the actual electrical zero position of the motor
- property AAMotion.AAValueArray<System.Double> ComtMode { get; }
  Commutation mode parameter array Index 1: Auto-phasing method 0: Jump to zero phase1: Reserved2: Absolute encoder + preset offset3: Special encoder and Hall switch4: Encoder and Hall/encoder switch5: Minimum jump search6: Hall commutation only Index 2: Voltage for each step of auto-phasing in jump to zero phase method Index 3: Duration for each step of auto-phasing in jump to zero phase method Index 4: Absolute encoder reading when defining zero electrical angle in auto-phasing based on absolute encoder Index 5: Set to 1282 to start auto-phasing process Index 6: Jump to zero phase auto-phasing process mode, 0 for jump, 1 for smooth (recommended) Index 7~17: Reserved Index 18: Required accuracy for auto-phasing completion Index 19: Event for auto-execution of auto-phasing 0: Automatic on power-up1: Manual request only2: Automatic when motor is enabled after power-up (if needed)3: Automatic on power-up and when motor is enabled Index 20~24: Minimum jump method auto-phasing process configuration
- property AAMotion.AAValueArray<System.Double> ComtStatus { get; }
  Actual status of the auto-phasing process. Index 1 indicates the status of the auto-phasing process, with the following values: 0: Not completed 1: In progress 100: Completed successfully 200: Not required 300: Completed successfully (using Hall) 400: Completed successfully (using Hall) 500: Completed successfully (parameters modified!!) 600: Virtual commutation in aging mode -1: Failed; motor unexpectedly disabled (see MotorReason command) -2: Failed; unknown auto-phasing method -3: Failed; search exceeded 360 degrees -4: Failed; encoder disconnected -5: Commutation required; related parameters modified -6: Failed; hardware not ready. Power cycle required -7: Failed; illegal Hall signal value. Power cycle required -8: Commutation required; Central-i remote unit disconnected -9: Failed; illegal Hall value (0 or 7) during self-learning -10: Failed; illegal Hall sequence during self-learning -11: Failed; illegal Hall value. EncDir modified -12: Failed; illegal Hall sequence. EncDir modified
- property System.String ConFaultMsg { get; }
  Controller fault information Current fault description information of the controller.

Interface ISingleAxis (AAMotion; AAMotion C#): Single axis interface, defines all properties and methods of a single axis motion controller. This is a sub-interface of IVector.
Signature: interface AAMotion.ISingleAxis

Members:
- property System.Int32 ConFlt { get; set; }
  ConFlt reports the error that caused the disabling of the motor. Every fault that is entered to ConFlt is also logged in ErrLog. ConFlt is cleared by the next motor enable. The current fault code of the controller. 0 indicates no fault.
